#DisorderCategory-2.APP-DisorderCategory
  .APP-ChapterIntro
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders include schizophrenia, other psychotic disorders, and schizotypal (personality) disorder. They are defined by abnormalities in one or more of the following five domains: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized thinking (speech), grossly disorganized or abnormal motor behavior (including catatonia), and negative symptoms.
  .sectionBar(id='x92567.2413001')
    .contentJumpLinks
      .contentNext
        a(href='#x92567.2413116')
          img.next(src='../../widgets/publication-content/images/article_section_next.png', alt='Next')
    h2.sectionHeader Key Features That Define the Psychotic Disorders
  section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2413043')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2411610') Delusions
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Delusions
      |  are fixed beliefs that are not amenable to change in light of conflicting evidence. Their content may include a variety of themes (e.g., persecutory, referential, somatic, religious, grandiose).
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Persecutory delusions
      |  (i.e., belief that one is going to be harmed, harassed, and so forth by an individual, organization, or other group) are most common.
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Referential delusions
      |  (i.e., belief that certain gestures, comments, environmental cues, and so forth are directed at oneself) are also common.
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Grandiose delusions
      |  (i.e., when an individual believes that he or she has exceptional abilities, wealth, or fame) and
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic erotomanic delusions
      |  (i.e., when an individual believes falsely that another person is in love with him or her) are also seen.
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Nihilistic delusions
      |  involve the conviction that a major catastrophe will occur, and
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic somatic delusions
      |  focus on preoccupations regarding health and organ function.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Delusions are deemed
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic bizarre
      |  if they are clearly implausible and not understandable to same-culture peers and do not derive from ordinary life experiences. An example of a bizarre delusion is the belief that an outside force has removed his or her internal organs and replaced them with someone else’s organs without leaving any wounds or scars. An example of a nonbizarre delusion is the belief that one is under surveillance by the police, despite a lack of convincing evidence. Delusions that express a loss of control over mind or body are generally considered to be bizarre; these include the belief that one’s thoughts have been “removed” by some outside force (
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic thought withdrawal
      | ), that alien thoughts have been put into one’s mind (
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic thought insertion
      | ), or that one’s body or actions are being acted on or manipulated by some outside force (
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic delusions of control
      | ). The distinction between a delusion and a strongly held idea is sometimes difficult to make and depends in part on the degree of conviction with which the belief is held despite clear or reasonable contradictory evidence regarding its veracity.
  section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2413060')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2411634') Hallucinations
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Hallucinations
      |  are perception-like experiences that occur without an external stimulus. They are vivid and clear, with the full force and impact of normal perceptions, and not under voluntary control. They may occur in any sensory modality, but auditory hallucinations are the most common in schizophrenia and related disorders. Auditory hallucinations are usually experienced as voices, whether familiar or unfamiliar, that are perceived as distinct from the individual’s own thoughts. The hallucinations must occur in the context of a clear sensorium; those that occur while falling asleep (
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic hypnagogic
      | ) or waking up (
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic hypnopompic
      | ) are considered to be within the range of normal experience. Hallucinations may be a normal part of religious experience in certain cultural contexts.
  section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2413072')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2411647') Disorganized Thinking (Speech)
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Disorganized thinking
      |  (
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic formal thought disorder
      | ) is typically inferred from the individual’s speech. The individual may switch from one topic to another (
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic derailment or loose associations
      | ). Answers to questions may be obliquely related or completely unrelated (
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic tangentiality
      | ). Rarely, speech may be so severely disorganized that it is nearly incomprehensible and resembles receptive aphasia in its linguistic disorganization (
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic incoherence
      |  or “word salad”). Because mildly disorganized speech is common and nonspecific, the symptom must be severe enough to substantially impair effective communication. The severity of the impairment may be difficult to evaluate if the person making the diagnosis comes from a different linguistic background than that of the person being examined. Less severe disorganized thinking or speech may occur during the prodromal and residual periods of schizophrenia.
  section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2413085')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2411662') Grossly Disorganized or Abnormal Motor Behavior (Including Catatonia)
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Grossly disorganized or abnormal motor behavior
      |  may manifest itself in a variety of ways, ranging from childlike “silliness” to unpredictable agitation. Problems may be noted in any form of goal-directed behavior, leading to difficulties in performing activities of daily living.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Catatonic behavior
      |  is a marked decrease in reactivity to the environment. This ranges from resistance to instructions (
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic negativism
      | ); to maintaining a rigid, inappropriate or bizarre posture; to a complete lack of verbal and motor responses (
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic mutism
      |  and
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic stupor
      | ). It can also include purposeless and excessive motor activity without obvious cause (
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic catatonic excitement
      | ). Other features are repeated stereotyped movements, staring, grimacing, mutism, and the echoing of speech. Although catatonia has historically been associated with schizophrenia, catatonic symptoms are nonspecific and may occur in other mental disorders (e.g., bipolar or depressive disorders with catatonia) and in medical conditions (catatonic disorder due to another medical condition).
  section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2413102')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2411684') Negative Symptoms
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Negative symptoms
      |  account for a substantial portion of the morbidity associated with schizophrenia but are less prominent in other psychotic disorders. Two negative symptoms are particularly prominent in schizophrenia: diminished emotional expression and avolition.
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Diminished emotional expression
      |  includes reductions in the expression of emotions in the face, eye contact, intonation of speech (prosody), and movements of the hand, head, and face that normally give an emotional emphasis to speech.
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Avolition
      |  is a decrease in motivated self-initiated purposeful activities. The individual may sit for long periods of time and show little interest in participating in work or social activities. Other negative symptoms include alogia, anhedonia, and asociality.
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Alogia
      |  is manifested by diminished speech output.
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Anhedonia
      |  is the decreased ability to experience pleasure from positive stimuli or a degradation in the recollection of pleasure previously experienced
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHBCJGA') Kring and Moran 2008
        | )
      | .
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Asociality
      |  refers to the apparent lack of interest in social interactions and may be associated with avolition, but it can also be a manifestation of limited opportunities for social interactions.
  .sectionBar(id='x92567.2413116')
