          i.fa.fa-arrow-up.delta-FT-deleted(aria-hidden='true')
          |  or
          i.fa.fa-arrow-up.delta-FT-deleted(aria-hidden='true')
          |  that parts of the body are not functioning.
  .APP-DiagnosticFeatures(id='x92567.2390458')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2390204') Diagnostic Features
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The essential feature of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
      |  is the presence of one or more delusions that persist for at least 1 month (Criterion A). A diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
      |  is not given if the individual has ever had a symptom presentation that met Criterion A for schizophrenia (Criterion B). Apart from the direct impact of the delusions, impairments in psychosocial functioning may be more circumscribed than those seen in other psychotic disorders such as schizophrenia, and behavior is not obviously bizarre or odd (Criterion C). If mood episodes occur concurrently with the delusions, the total duration of these mood episodes is brief relative to the total duration of the delusional periods (Criterion D). The delusions are not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., cocaine) or another medical condition (e.g., Alzheimer’s disease) and are not better explained by another mental disorder, such as body dysmorphic disorder or obsessive-compulsive disorder (Criterion E).
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | In addition to the five symptom domain areas identified in the diagnostic criteria, the assessment of cognition, depression, and mania symptom domains is vital for making critically important distinctions between the various schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders.
  .APP-AssociatedFeatures(id='x92567.2390465')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2390217') Associated Features Supporting Diagnosis
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Social, marital, or work problems can result from the delusional beliefs of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJHCBC') de Portugal et al. 2011
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHIJEHD') Kendler 1982
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHBDGHD') Kendler and Walsh 1995
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHEHHAC') Marneros et al. 2012
        | )
      | . Individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
      |  may be able to factually describe that others view their beliefs as irrational but are unable to accept this themselves (i.e., there may be “factual insight” but no true insight). Many individuals develop irritable or dysphoric mood, which can usually be understood as a reaction to their delusional beliefs
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFHEAC') de Portugal et al. 2008
        | )
      | . Anger and violent behavior can occur with persecutory, jealous, and erotomanic types. The individual may engage in litigious or antagonistic behavior (e.g., sending hundreds of letters of protest to the government). Legal difficulties can occur, particularly in jealous and erotomanic types.
  h3.Head(id='x92567.2390234') Prevalence
  p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
    | The lifetime prevalence of
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
    |  has been estimated at around 0.2%
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHEDEHG') Perälä et al. 2007
      | )
    | , and the most frequent subtype is persecutory
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite de Portugal et al. 2010
      | )
    | .
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName Delusional disorder, jealous type
    | , is probably more common in males than in females, but there are no major gender differences in the overall frequency of
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCJDDA') de Portugal et al. 2010
      | ;
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHIJEHD') Kendler 1982
      | ;
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHEHHAC') Marneros et al. 2012
      | ;
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCCBBC') Wustmann et al. 2011
      | )
    | .
  .APP-DevelopmentAndCourse(id='x92567.2390489')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2390253') Development and Course
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | On average, global function is generally better than that observed in
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFHEAC') de Portugal et al. 2008
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCJDDA') de Portugal et al. 2010
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJHCBC') de Portugal et al. 2011
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHIJEHD') Kendler 1982
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHBDGHD') Kendler and Walsh 1995
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHEHHAC') Marneros et al. 2012
        | )
      | . Although the diagnosis is generally stable, a proportion of individuals go on to develop schizophrenia
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHEHHAC') Marneros et al. 2012
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCDDGJ') Salvatore et al. 2011
        | )
      | .
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName Delusional disorder
      |  has a significant familial relationship with both
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  and
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizotypal personality disorder
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHHBCII') Kendler et al. 1993
        | )
      | . Although it can occur in younger age groups, the condition may be more prevalent in older individuals
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCJDDA') de Portugal et al. 2010
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHIJEHD') Kendler 1982
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHEHHAC') Marneros et al. 2012
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCCBBC') Wustmann et al. 2011
        | )
      | .
  .APP-CultureRelatedDiagnosticIssues(id='x92567.2390509')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2390279') Culture-Related Diagnostic Issues
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | An individual’s cultural and religious background must be taken into account in evaluating the possible presence of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
      | . The content of delusions also varies across cultural contexts.
  .APP-FunctionalConsequences(id='x92567.2390515')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2390291') Functional Consequences of Delusional Disorder
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The functional impairment is usually more circumscribed than that seen with other psychotic disorders, although in some cases, the impairment may be substantial and include poor occupational functioning and social isolation
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFHEAC') de Portugal et al. 2008
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJHCBC') de Portugal et al. 2011
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHBDGHD') Kendler and Walsh 1995
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHEHHAC') Marneros et al. 2012
        | )
      | . When poor psychosocial functioning is present, delusional beliefs themselves often play a significant role. A common characteristic of individuals with delusional disorder is the apparent normality of their behavior and appearance when their delusional ideas are not being discussed or acted on.
  .APP-DifferentialDiagnosis(id='x92567.2390524')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2390306') Differential Diagnosis
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2390390')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2390311') Obsessive-compulsive and related disorders
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | If an individual with
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName obsessive-compulsive disorder
        |  is completely convinced that his or her
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName obsessive-compulsive disorder
        |  beliefs are true, then the diagnosis of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName obsessive-compulsive disorder, with absent insight/delusional beliefs specifier
        | , should be given rather than a diagnosis of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
        | . Similarly, if an individual with
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName body dysmorphic disorder
        |  is completely convinced that his or her
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName body dysmorphic disorder
        |  beliefs are true, then the diagnosis of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName body dysmorphic disorder, with absent insight/delusional beliefs specifier
        | , should be given rather than a diagnosis of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
        | .
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2390406')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2390333')
        | Delirium, major neurocognitive disorder, psychotic disorder due to another medical condition, and substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Individuals with these disorders may present with symptoms that suggest
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
        | . For example, simple persecutory delusions in the context of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName major neurocognitive disorder
        |  would be diagnosed as
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName major neurocognitive disorder, with behavioral disturbance
        | . A
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder
        |  cross-sectionally may be identical in symptomatology to
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
        |  but can be distinguished by the chronological relationship of substance use to the onset and remission of the delusional beliefs.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2390416')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2390349') Schizophrenia and schizophreniform disorder
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName Delusional disorder
        |  can be distinguished from
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  and
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
        |  by the absence of the other characteristic symptoms of the active phase of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        | .
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2390424')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2390362') Depressive and bipolar disorders and schizoaffective disorder
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | These disorders may be distinguished from
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
        |  by the temporal relationship between the mood disturbance and the delusions and by the severity of the mood symptoms. If delusions occur exclusively during mood episodes, the diagnosis is depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features. Mood symptoms that meet full criteria for a mood episode can be superimposed on
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJHCBC') de Portugal et al. 2011
          | )
        | .
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName Delusional disorder
        |  can be diagnosed only if the total duration of all mood episodes remains brief relative to the total duration of the delusional disturbance. If not, then a diagnosis of other specified or unspecified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder accompanied by other specified depressive disorder, unspecified depressive disorder, other specified bipolar and related disorder, or unspecified bipolar and related disorder is appropriate.
