    h2.sectionHeader Brief Psychotic Disorder
  .APP-CriteriaSet(id='x92567.1778826')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.1778677') Diagnostic Criteria
    span.APP-ICDCode.rightICDCode
      span.APP-ICD9Code 298.8
      span.ICD10Code (F23)
    ol.APP-List.APP-AlphaUpper(start='1')
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | Presence of one (or more) of the following symptoms. At least one of these must be (1), (2), or (3):
        ol.APP-List.APP-NumberedLT10(start='1')
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Delusions.
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Hallucinations.
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Disorganized speech (e.g., frequent derailment or incoherence).
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior.
        .APP-CriteriaSetNote
          ul.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-UnNumberedUnindented
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
                b Note:
                | Do not include a symptom if it is a culturally sanctioned response.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | Duration of an episode of the disturbance is at least 1 day but less than 1 month, with eventual full return to premorbid level of functioning.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | The disturbance is not better explained by
          span.APP-DSMDisorderName major depressive
          |  or
          span.APP-DSMDisorderName bipolar disorder with psychotic features
          span.APP-DSMDisorderName or another psychotic disorder such
          | as
          span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
          |  or catatonia, and is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., a drug of abuse, a medication) or another medical condition.
    .APP-NoteIntro
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
      |  if:
    ul.APP-List.APP-UnNumbered
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          b With marked stressor(s)
          |  (brief reactive psychosis): If symptoms occur in response to events that, singly or together, would be markedly stressful to almost anyone in similar circumstances in the individual’s culture.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          b Without marked stressor(s):
          |  If symptoms do not occur in response to events that, singly or together, would be markedly stressful to almost anyone in similar circumstances in the individual’s culture.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          b With peripartum onset:
          |  If onset is during pregnancy or within 4 weeks postpartum.
    .APP-NoteIntro
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
      |  if:
    ul.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-UnNumbered
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          b With catatonia
          |  (refer to the criteria for catatonia associated with another mental disorder, pp. 119–120, for definition)
        .APP-CodeNote
          ul.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-UnNumberedIndented
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
                b Coding note:
                |  Use additional code 293.89 (F06.1) catatonia associated with brief psychotic disorder to indicate the presence of the comorbid catatonia.
    .APP-SeveritySpecifierNote
      .APP-NoteIntro
        span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
        |  current severity:
      ul.APP-List.APP-UnNumbered
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            | Severity is rated by a quantitative assessment of the primary symptoms of psychosis, including delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, abnormal psychomotor behavior, and negative symptoms. Each of these symptoms may be rated for its current severity (most severe in the last 7 days) on a 5-point scale ranging from 0 (not present) to 4 (present and severe). (See Clinician-Rated Dimensions of Psychosis Symptom Severity in the chapter “Assessment Measures.”)
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Note:
            |  Diagnosis of brief psychotic disorder can be made without using this severity specifier.
  .APP-DiagnosticFeatures(id='x92567.1769879')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.1769488') Diagnostic Features
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The essential feature of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
      |  is a disturbance that involves the sudden onset of at least one of the following positive psychotic symptoms: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech (e.g., frequent derailment or incoherence), or grossly abnormal psychomotor behavior, including catatonia (Criterion A).
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Sudden onset
      |  is defined as change from a nonpsychotic state to a clearly psychotic state within 2 weeks, usually without a prodrome. An episode of the disturbance lasts at least 1 day but less than 1 month, and the individual eventually has a full return to the premorbid level of functioning (Criterion B). The disturbance is not better explained by a
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features
      | , by
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | , or by
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  and is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., a hallucinogen) or another medical condition (e.g., subdural hematoma) (Criterion C).
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | In addition to the five symptom domain areas identified in the diagnostic criteria, the assessment of cognition, depression, and mania symptom domains is vital for making critically important distinctions between the various schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders.
  .APP-AssociatedFeatures(id='x92567.1769889')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.1769504') Associated Features Supporting Diagnosis
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
      |  typically experience emotional turmoil or overwhelming confusion
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHDHAHA') Fochtmann et al. 2009
        | )
      | . They may have rapid shifts from one intense affect to another. Although the disturbance is brief, the level of impairment may be severe, and supervision may be required to ensure that nutritional and hygienic needs are met and that the individual is protected from the consequences of poor judgment, cognitive impairment, or acting on the basis of delusions. There appears to be an increased risk of suicidal behavior, particularly during the acute episode
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHDIACC') Pillmann et al. 2003
        | )
      | .
  h3.Head(id='x92567.1769517') Prevalence
  p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
    | In the United States,
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
    |  may account for 9% of cases of first-onset psychosis
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCIBHC') Susser et al. 1995
      | )
    | . Psychotic disturbances that meet Criteria A and C, but not Criterion B, for
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
    |  (i.e., duration of active symptoms is 1–6 months as opposed to remission within 1 month) are more common in developing countries than in developed countries
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCHBDC') Susser and Wanderling 1994
      | )
    | .
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName  Brief psychotic disorder
    |  is twofold more common in females than in males
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCHBDC') Susser and Wanderling 1994
      | )
    | .
  .APP-DevelopmentAndCourse(id='x92567.1769905')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.1769532') Development and Course
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName Brief psychotic disorder
      |  may appear in adolescence or early adulthood, and onset can occur across the lifespan, with the average age at onset being the mid 30s
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHECDBF') Pillmann et al. 2002
        | )
      | . By definition, a diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
      |  requires a full remission of all symptoms and an eventual full return to the premorbid level of functioning within 1 month of the onset of the disturbance. In some individuals, the duration of psychotic symptoms may be quite brief (e.g., a few days).
  .APP-RiskAndPrognosticFactors(id='x92567.1769912')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.1769544') Risk and Prognostic Factors
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.1778495')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.1768467') Temperamental
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Preexisting personality disorders and traits (e.g.,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizotypal personality disorder;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName borderline personality disorder
        | ; or traits in the psychoticism domain, such as perceptual dysregulation, and the negative affectivity domain, such as suspiciousness) may predispose the individual to the development of the disorder.
  .APP-CultureRelatedDiagnosticIssues(id='x92567.1769919')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.1769556') Culture-Related Diagnostic Issues
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | It is important to distinguish symptoms of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
      |  from culturally sanctioned response patterns. For example, in some religious ceremonies, an individual may report hearing voices, but these do not generally persist and are not perceived as abnormal by most members of the individual’s community. In addition, cultural and religious background must be taken into account when considering whether beliefs are delusional.
  .APP-FunctionalConsequences(id='x92567.1769937')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.1769591') Functional Consequences of Brief Psychotic Disorder
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Despite high rates of relapse, for most individuals, outcome is excellent in terms of social functioning and symptomatology
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHECDBF') Pillmann et al. 2002
        | )
      | .
  .APP-DifferentialDiagnosis(id='x92567.1769943')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.1769603') Differential Diagnosis
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.1769802')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.1769638') Other medical conditions
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | A variety of medical disorders can manifest with psychotic symptoms of short duration.
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName Psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
        |  or a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delirium
        |  is diagnosed when there is evidence from the history, physical examination, or laboratory tests that the delusions or hallucinations are the direct physiological consequence of a specific medical condition (e.g., Cushing’s syndrome, brain tumor) (see “
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName Psychotic Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition”
        |  later in this chapter).
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.1769810')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.1769652') Substance-related disorders
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName Substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder
        | ,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance-induced delirium
        | , and
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance intoxication
        |  are distinguished from
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
        |  by the fact that a substance (e.g., a drug of abuse, a medication, exposure to a toxin) is judged to be etiologically related to the psychotic symptoms (see “
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName Substance/Medication-Induced Psychotic Disorder
        | ” later in this chapter). Laboratory tests, such as a urine drug screen or a blood alcohol level, may be helpful in making this determination, as may a careful history of substance use with attention to temporal relationships between substance intake and onset of the symptoms and to the nature of the substance being used.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.1769819')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.1769666') Depressive and bipolar disorders
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | The diagnosis of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
        |  cannot be made if the psychotic symptoms are better explained by a mood episode (i.e., the psychotic symptoms occur exclusively during a full
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName major depressive
        | ,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName manic
        | , or
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName mixed episode
        | ).
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.1769828')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.1769681') Other psychotic disorders
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | If the psychotic symptoms persist for 1 month or longer, the diagnosis is either
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
        | ,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
        | ,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName depressive disorder with psychotic features
        | ,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName bipolar disorder with psychotic features
        | , or other specified or unspecified schizophrenia spectrum and other
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder
        | , depending on the other symptoms in the presentation. The differential diagnosis between
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
        |  and
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
        |  is difficult when the psychotic symptoms have remitted before 1 month in response to successful treatment with medication. Careful attention should be given to the possibility that a recurrent disorder (e.g.,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName bipolar disorder
        | , recurrent acute exacerbations of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        | ) may be responsible for any recurring psychotic episodes.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.1769842')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.1769701') Malingering and factitious disorders
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | An episode of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName factitious disorder, with predominantly psychological signs and symptoms
        | , may have the appearance of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
        | , but in such cases there is evidence that the symptoms are intentionally produced. When
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName malingering
        |  involves apparently psychotic symptoms, there is usually evidence that the illness is being feigned for an understandable goal.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.1769850')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.1769715') Personality disorders
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | In certain individuals with
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName personality disorders
        | , psychosocial stressors may precipitate brief periods of psychotic symptoms
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite Jorgensen et al. 1996
          | )
        | . These symptoms are usually transient and do not warrant a separate diagnosis. If psychotic symptoms persist for at least 1 day, an additional diagnosis of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
        |  may be appropriate.
