    h2.sectionHeader Schizophreniform Disorder
  .APP-CriteriaSet(id='x92567.2410163')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2446948') Diagnostic Criteria
    span.APP-ICDCode.rightICDCode
      | 295.40
      span.APP-ICD9Code
      span.ICD10Code (F20.81)
    ol.APP-List.APP-AlphaUpper(start='1')
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | Two (or more) of the following, each present for a significant portion of time during a 1-month period (or less if successfully treated). At least one of these must be (1), (2), or (3):
        ol.APP-List.APP-NumberedLT10(start='1')
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Delusions.
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Hallucinations.
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Disorganized speech (e.g., frequent derailment or incoherence).
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior.
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Negative symptoms (i.e., diminished emotional expression or avolition).
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | An episode of the disorder lasts at least 1 month but less than 6 months. When the diagnosis must be made without waiting for recovery, it should be qualified as “provisional.”
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | Schizoaffective disorder and depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features have been ruled out because either 1) no major depressive or manic episodes have occurred concurrently with the active-phase symptoms, or 2) if mood episodes have occurred during active-phase symptoms, they have been present for a minority of the total duration of the active and residual periods of the illness.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | The disturbance is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., a drug of abuse, a medication) or another medical condition.
    .APP-NoteIntro
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
      |  if:
    ul.APP-List.APP-UnNumbered
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          b With good prognostic features:
          |  This specifier requires the presence of at least two of the following features: onset of prominent psychotic symptoms within 4 weeks of the first noticeable change in usual behavior or functioning; confusion or perplexity; good premorbid social and occupational functioning; and absence of blunted or flat affect.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          b Without good prognostic features:
          |  This specifier is applied if two or more of the above features have not been present.
    .APP-NoteIntro
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
      |  if:
    ul.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-UnNumbered
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          b With catatonia
          |  (refer to the criteria for catatonia associated with another mental disorder, pp. 119–120, for definition).
        .APP-CodeNote
          ul.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-UnNumberedIndented
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
                b Coding note:
                |  Use additional code 293.89 (F06.1) catatonia associated with schizophreniform disorder to indicate the presence of the comorbid catatonia.
    .APP-SeveritySpecifierNote
      .APP-NoteIntro
        span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
        |  current severity:
      ul.APP-List.APP-UnNumbered
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            | Severity is rated by a quantitative assessment of the primary symptoms of psychosis, including delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, abnormal psychomotor behavior, and negative symptoms. Each of these symptoms may be rated for its current severity (most severe in the last 7 days) on a 5-point scale ranging from 0 (not present) to 4 (present and severe). (See Clinician-Rated Dimensions of Psychosis Symptom Severity in the chapter “Assessment Measures.”)
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Note:
            |  Diagnosis of schizophreniform disorder can be made without using this severity specifier.
  .APP-DisorderNote
    b Note:
    |  For additional information on Associated Features Supporting Diagnosis, Development and Course (age-related factors), Culture-Related Diagnostic Issues, Gender-Related Diagnostic Issues, Differential Diagnosis, and Comorbidity, see the corresponding sections in schizophrenia.
  .APP-DiagnosticFeatures(id='x92567.2410872')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2410646') Diagnostic Features
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The characteristic symptoms of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
      |  are identical to those of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  (Criterion A).
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName Schizophreniform disorder
      |  is distinguished by its difference in duration: the total duration of the illness, including prodromal, active, and residual phases, is at least 1 month but less than 6 months (Criterion B). The duration requirement for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
      |  is intermediate between that for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
      | , which lasts more than 1 day and remits by 1 month, and
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | , which lasts for at least 6 months. The diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
      |  is made under two conditions: 1) when an episode of illness lasts between 1 and 6 months and the individual has already recovered, and 2) when an individual is symptomatic for less than the 6 months’ duration required for the diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  but has not yet recovered. In this case, the diagnosis should be noted as “schizophreniform disorder (provisional)” because it is uncertain if the individual will recover from the disturbance within the 6-month period. If the disturbance persists beyond 6 months, the diagnosis should be changed to
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | .
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Another distinguishing feature of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
      |  is the lack of a criterion requiring impaired social and occupational functioning. While such impairments may potentially be present, they are not necessary for a diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
      | .
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | In addition to the five symptom domain areas identified in the diagnostic criteria, the assessment of cognition, depression, and mania symptom domains is vital for making critically important distinctions between the various schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders.
  .APP-AssociatedFeatures(id='x92567.2410889')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2410674') Associated Features Supporting Diagnosis
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | As with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | , currently there are no laboratory or psychometric tests for schizophreniform disorder. There are multiple brain regions where neuroimaging, neuropathological, and neurophysiological research has indicated abnormalities, but none are diagnostic
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFCIFB') Minzenberg et al. 2009
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJDCBE') Ragland et al. 2009
        | )
      | .
  h3.Head(id='x92567.2422131') Prevalence
  p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
    | Incidence of
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
    |  across sociocultural settings is likely similar to that observed in
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHHJICJ') Naz et al. 2003
      | )
    | . In the United States and other developed countries, the incidence is low, possibly fivefold less than that of
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHDGHDB') Baldwin et al. 2005
      | ;
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJGDAJ') Bromet et al. 2011
      | )
    | . In developing countries, the incidence may be higher, especially for the specifier “with good prognostic features”; in some of these settings
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
    |  may be as common as
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
    | .
  .APP-DevelopmentAndCourse(id='x92567.2410908')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2410705') Development and Course
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The development of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
      |  is similar to that of schizophrenia. About one-third of individuals with an initial diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder (provisional)
      |  recover within the 6-month period and schizophreniform disorder is their final diagnosis
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHHJICJ') Naz et al. 2003
        | )
      | . The majority of the remaining two-thirds of individuals will eventually receive a diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  or
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJGDAJ') Bromet et al. 2011
        | )
      | .
  .APP-RiskAndPrognosticFactors(id='x92567.2410918')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2410721') Risk and Prognostic Factors
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2410827')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2410726') Genetic and physiological
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Relatives of individuals with
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
        |  have an increased risk for
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFJCHC') Kendler and Walsh
            |  1995
          | )
        | .
  .APP-FunctionalConsequences(id='x92567.2410925')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2410739') Functional Consequences of Schizophreniform Disorder
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | For the majority of individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
      |  who eventually receive a diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  or
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | , the functional consequences are similar to the consequences of those disorders. Most individuals experience dysfunction in several areas of daily functioning, such as school or work, interpersonal relationships, and self-care. Individuals who recover from
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
      |  have better functional outcomes.
  .APP-DifferentialDiagnosis(id='x92567.2410934')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2422311') Differential Diagnosis
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2410834')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2410759') Other mental disorders and medical conditions
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | A wide variety of mental and medical conditions can manifest with psychotic symptoms that must be considered in the differential diagnosis of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
        | . These include
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
        |  or its treatment;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delirium
        |  or
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName major neurocognitive disorder
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder
        |  or
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delirium
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName depressive or
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName bipolar disorder with psychotic features
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName other specified or unspecified bipolar and related disorder
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName depressive or bipolar disorder with catatonic features
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName
          | other specified or unspecified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizotypal, schizoid, or paranoid personality disorders
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName autism spectrum disorder
        | ; disorders presenting in childhood with disorganized speech;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName obsessive-compulsive disorder
        | ;
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName posttraumatic stress disorder
        | ; and
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName traumatic brain injury
        | .
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Since the diagnostic criteria for
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder and
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  differ primarily in duration of illness, the discussion of the differential diagnosis of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  also applies to
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder
        | .
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2410866')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2410807') Brief psychotic disorder
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName Schizophreniform disorder
        |  differs in duration from
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder,
        | which has a duration of less than 1 month.
