    h2.sectionHeader Schizophrenia
  .APP-CriteriaSet(id='x92567.2429704')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2429707') Diagnostic Criteria
    span.APP-ICDCode.rightICDCode
      span.APP-ICD9Code 295.90
      span.ICD10Code (F20.9)
    ol.APP-List.APP-AlphaUpper(start='1')
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | Two (or more) of the following, each present for a significant portion of time during a 1-month period (or less if successfully treated). At least one of these must be (1), (2), or (3):
        ol.APP-List.APP-NumberedLT10(start='1')
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Delusions.
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Hallucinations.
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Disorganized speech (e.g., frequent derailment or incoherence).
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior.
          li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
            p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Negative symptoms (i.e., diminished emotional expression or avolition).
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | For a significant portion of the time since the onset of the disturbance, level of functioning in one or more major areas, such as work, interpersonal relations, or self-care, is markedly below the level achieved prior to the onset (or when the onset is in childhood or adolescence, there is failure to achieve expected level of interpersonal, academic, or occupational functioning).
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | Continuous signs of the disturbance persist for at least 6 months. This 6-month period must include at least 1 month of symptoms (or less if successfully treated) that meet Criterion A (i.e., active-phase symptoms) and may include periods of prodromal or residual symptoms. During these prodromal or residual periods, the signs of the disturbance may be manifested by only negative symptoms or by two or more symptoms listed in Criterion A present in an attenuated form (e.g., odd beliefs, unusual perceptual experiences).
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | Schizoaffective disorder and depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features have been ruled out because either 1) no major depressive or manic episodes have occurred concurrently with the active-phase symptoms, or 2) if mood episodes have occurred during active-phase symptoms, they have been present for a minority of the total duration of the active and residual periods of the illness.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | The disturbance is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., a drug of abuse, a medication) or another medical condition.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | If there is a history of autism spectrum disorder or a communication disorder of childhood onset, the additional diagnosis of schizophrenia is made only if prominent delusions or hallucinations, in addition to the other required symptoms of schizophrenia, are also present for at least 1 month (or less if successfully treated).
    .APP-CourseSpecifierNote
      .APP-NoteIntro
        span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
        | if:
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | The following course specifiers are only to be used after a 1-year duration of the disorder and if they are not in contradiction to the diagnostic course criteria.
      ul.APP-List.APP-UnNumbered
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b First episode, currently in acute episode:
            |  First manifestation of the disorder meeting the defining diagnostic symptom and time criteria. An
            span.APP-EmphasisItalic acute episode
            |  is a time period in which the symptom criteria are fulfilled.
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b First episode, currently in partial remission:
            span.APP-EmphasisItalic Partial remission
            |  is a period of time during which an improvement after a previous episode is maintained and in which the defining criteria of the disorder are only partially fulfilled.
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b First episode, currently in full remission:
            span.APP-EmphasisItalic Full remission
            |  is a period of time after a previous episode during which no disorder-specific symptoms are present.
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Multiple episodes, currently in acute episode:
            |  Multiple episodes may be determined after a minimum of two episodes (i.e., after a first episode, a remission and a minimum of one relapse).
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Multiple episodes, currently in partial remission
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Multiple episodes, currently in full remission
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Continuous:
            |  Symptoms fulfilling the diagnostic symptom criteria of the disorder are remaining for the majority of the illness course, with subthreshold symptom periods being very brief relative to the overall course.
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Unspecified
    .APP-NoteIntro
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
      |  if:
    ul.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-UnNumbered
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          b With catatonia
          |  (refer to the criteria for catatonia associated with another mental disorder, pp. 119–120, for definition).
        .APP-CodeNote
          ul.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-UnNumberedIndented
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
                b Coding note:
                |  Use additional code 293.89 (F06.1) catatonia associated with schizophrenia to indicate the presence of the comorbid catatonia.
    .APP-SeveritySpecifierNote
      .APP-NoteIntro
        span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
        |  current severity:
      ul.APP-List.APP-UnNumbered
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            | Severity is rated by a quantitative assessment of the primary symptoms of psychosis, including delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, abnormal psychomotor behavior, and negative symptoms. Each of these symptoms may be rated for its current severity (most severe in the last 7 days) on a 5-point scale ranging from 0 (not present) to 4 (present and severe). (See Clinician-Rated Dimensions of Psychosis Symptom Severity in the chapter “Assessment Measures.”)
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Note:
            |  Diagnosis of schizophrenia can be made without using this severity specifier.
  .APP-DiagnosticFeatures(id='x92567.2414712')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2414166') Diagnostic Features
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The characteristic symptoms of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  involve a range of cognitive, behavioral, and emotional dysfunctions, but no single symptom is pathognomonic of the disorder. The diagnosis involves the recognition of a constellation of signs and symptoms associated with impaired occupational or social functioning. Individuals with the disorder will vary substantially on most features, as
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  is a heterogeneous clinical syndrome.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | At least two Criterion A symptoms must be present for a significant portion of time during a 1-month period or longer. At least one of these symptoms must be the clear presence of delusions (Criterion A1), hallucinations (Criterion A2), or disorganized speech (Criterion A3). Grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior (Criterion A4) and negative symptoms (Criterion A5) may also be present. In those situations in which the active-phase symptoms remit within a month in response to treatment, Criterion A is still met if the clinician estimates that they would have persisted in the absence of treatment.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Schizophrenia involves impairment in one or more major areas of functioning (Criterion B). If the disturbance begins in childhood or adolescence, the expected level of function is not attained. Comparing the individual with unaffected siblings may be helpful. The dysfunction persists for a substantial period during the course of the disorder and does not appear to be a direct result of any single feature. Avolition (i.e., reduced drive to pursue goal-directed behavior; Criterion A5) is linked to the social dysfunction described under Criterion B. There is also strong evidence for a relationship between cognitive impairment (see the section “Associated Features Supporting Diagnosis” for this disorder) and functional impairment in individuals with schizophrenia.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Some signs of the disturbance must persist for a continuous period of at least 6 months (Criterion C). Prodromal symptoms often precede the active phase, and residual symptoms may follow it, characterized by mild or subthreshold forms of hallucinations or delusions. Individuals may express a variety of unusual or odd beliefs that are not of delusional proportions (e.g., ideas of reference or magical thinking); they may have unusual perceptual experiences (e.g., sensing the presence of an unseen person); their speech may be generally understandable but vague; and their behavior may be unusual but not grossly disorganized (e.g., mumbling in public). Negative symptoms are common in the prodromal and residual phases and can be severe. Individuals who had been socially active may become withdrawn from previous routines. Such behaviors are often the first sign of a disorder.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Mood symptoms and full mood episodes are common in
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  and may be concurrent with active-phase symptomatology. However, as distinct from a psychotic mood disorder, a
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  diagnosis requires the presence of delusions or hallucinations in the absence of mood episodes. In addition, mood episodes, taken in total, should be present for only a minority of the total duration of the active and residual periods of the illness.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | In addition to the five symptom domain areas identified in the diagnostic criteria, the assessment of cognition, depression, and mania symptom domains is vital for making critically important distinctions between the various schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders.
  .APP-AssociatedFeatures(id='x92567.2414719')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2414207') Associated Features Supporting Diagnosis
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  may display inappropriate affect (e.g., laughing in the absence of an appropriate stimulus); a dysphoric mood that can take the form of depression, anxiety, or anger; a disturbed sleep pattern (e.g., daytime sleeping and nighttime activity); and a lack of interest in eating or food refusal. Depersonalization, derealization, and somatic concerns may occur and sometimes reach delusional proportions. Anxiety and phobias are common
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHIBAEB') Tandon et al. 2009
        | )
      | . Cognitive deficits in
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  are common and are strongly linked to vocational and functional impairments. These deficits can include decrements in declarative memory, working memory, language function, and other executive functions, as well as slower processing speed
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHICIDB') Mesholam-Gately et al. 2009
        | )
      | . Abnormalities in sensory processing and inhibitory capacity, as well as reductions in attention, are also found. Some individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  show social cognition deficits, including deficits in the ability to infer the intentions of other people (theory of mind)
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJGDHE') Bora et al. 2009
        | )
      | , and may attend to and then interpret irrelevant events or stimuli as meaningful, perhaps leading to the generation of explanatory delusions. These impairments frequently persist during symptomatic remission.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Some individuals with psychosis may lack insight or awareness of their disorder (i.e., anosognosia). This lack of “insight” includes unawareness of symptoms of schizophrenia and may be present throughout the entire course of the illness. Unawareness of illness is typically a symptom of schizophrenia itself rather than a coping strategy. It is comparable to the lack of awareness of neurological deficits following brain damage, termed
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic anosognosia.
      |  This symptom is the most common predictor of non-adherence to treatment, and it predicts higher relapse rates, increased number of involuntary treatments, poorer psychosocial functioning, aggression, and a poorer course of illness
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHBABCJ') Shad et al. 2006
        | )
      | .
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Hostility and aggression can be associated with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | , although spontaneous or random assault is uncommon. Aggression is more frequent for younger males and for individuals with a past history of violence, non-adherence with treatment, substance abuse, and impulsivity
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCBHFI') Elbogen and Johnson 2009
        | )
      | . It should be noted that the vast majority of persons with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  are not aggressive and are more frequently victimized than are individuals in the general population
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHICFHJ') Teplin et al. 2005
        | )
      | .
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Currently, there are no radiological, laboratory, or psychometric tests for the disorder. Differences are evident in multiple brain regions between groups of healthy individuals and persons with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | , including evidence from neuroimaging, neuropathological, and neurophysiological studies
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHBDIHG') Minzenberg et al.
          |  2009
        | )
      | . Differences are also evident in cellular architecture, white matter connectivity, and gray matter volume in a variety of regions such as the prefrontal and temporal cortices
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFJIJC') Bora et al. 2011
        | )
      | . Reduced overall brain volume has been observed
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHGAJCH') Steen et al. 2006
        | )
      | , as well as increased brain volume reduction with age
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFDHBA') Olabi et al. 2011
        | )
      | . Brain volume reductions with age are more pronounced in individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  than in healthy individuals
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFDHBA') Olabi et al. 2011
        | )
      | . Finally, individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  appear to differ from individuals without the disorder in eye-tracking
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHGBBIG') O'Driscoll and Callahan 2008
        | )
      |  and electrophysiological indices
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHEGBCC') Jeon and Polich 2003
        | )
      | .
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Neurological soft signs common in individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  include impairments in motor coordination, sensory integration, and motor sequencing of complex movements; left-right confusion; and disinhibition of associated movements. In addition, minor physical anomalies of the face and limbs may occur
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCIDHG') Weinberg et al. 2007
        | )
      | .
  h3.Head(id='x92567.2414271') Prevalence
  p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
    | The lifetime prevalence of schizophrenia appears to be approximately 0.3%–0.7%
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHDJGEH') McGrath et al. 2008
      | )
    | , although there is reported variation by race/ethnicity, across countries, and by geographic origin for immigrants and children of immigrants. The sex ratio differs across samples and populations: for example, an emphasis on negative symptoms and longer duration of disorder (associated with poorer outcome) shows higher incidence rates for males
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHGDAFC') Roy et al. 2001
      | )
    | , whereas definitions allowing for the inclusion of more mood symptoms and brief presentations (associated with better outcome) show equivalent risks for both sexes
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHEGDJH') Beauchamp and Gagnon 2004
      | )
    | .
  .APP-DevelopmentAndCourse(id='x92567.2430424')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2414285') Development and Course
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The psychotic features of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  typically emerge between the late teens and the mid-30s; onset prior to adolescence is rare. The peak age at onset for the first psychotic episode is in the early- to mid-20s for males and in the late-20s for females
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHDJGEH') McGrath et al. 2008
        | )
      | . The onset may be abrupt or insidious, but the majority of individuals manifest a slow and gradual development of a variety of clinically significant signs and symptoms. Half of these individuals complain of depressive symptoms. Earlier age at onset has traditionally been seen as a predictor of worse prognosis. However, the effect of age at onset is likely related to gender, with males having worse premorbid adjustment, lower educational achievement, more prominent negative symptoms and cognitive impairment, and in general a worse outcome
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCGEHJ') Álvarez-Jiménez et al. 2012
        | )
      | . Impaired cognition is common, and alterations in cognition are present during development and precede the emergence of psychosis, taking the form of stable cognitive impairments during adulthood
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJDGAH') Tarbox and Pogue-Geile 2008
        | )
      | . Cognitive impairments may persist when other symptoms are in remission and contribute to the disability of the disease.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The predictors of course and outcome are largely unexplained, and course and outcome may not be reliably predicted. The course appears to be favorable in about 20% of those with schizophrenia, and a small number of individuals are reported to recover completely. However, most individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  still require formal or informal daily living supports, and many remain chronically ill, with exacerbations and remissions of active symptoms, while others have a course of progressive deterioration.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Psychotic symptoms tend to diminish over the life course, perhaps in association with normal age-related declines in dopamine activity. Negative symptoms are more closely related to prognosis than are positive symptoms and tend to be the most persistent
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCGFFC') Tamminga et al. 1998
        | )
      | . Furthermore, cognitive deficits associated with the illness may not improve over the course of the illness.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The essential features of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  are the same in childhood, but it is more difficult to make the diagnosis. In children, delusions and hallucinations may be less elaborate than in adults, and visual hallucinations are more common and should be distinguished from normal fantasy play. Disorganized speech occurs in many disorders with childhood onset (e.g.,
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName autism spectrum disorder
      | ), as does disorganized behavior (e.g.,
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder
      | ). These symptoms should not be attributed to
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  without due consideration of the more common disorders of childhood. Childhood-onset cases tend to resemble poor-outcome adult cases, with gradual onset and prominent negative symptoms. Children who later receive the diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  are more likely to have experienced nonspecific emotional-behavioral disturbances and psychopathology, intellectual and language alterations, and subtle motor delays.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Late-onset cases (i.e., onset after age 40 years) are overrepresented by females, who may have married
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHIBFHB') Howard et al. 2000
        | )
      | . Often, the course is characterized by a predominance of psychotic symptoms with preservation of affect and social functioning. Such late-onset cases can still meet the diagnostic criteria for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | , but it is not yet clear whether this is the same condition as
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  diagnosed prior to mid-life (e.g., prior to age 55 years).
  .APP-RiskAndPrognosticFactors(id='x92567.2414783')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2414339') Risk and Prognostic Factors
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2414623')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2414356') Environmental
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Season of birth has been linked to the incidence of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        | , including late winter/early spring in some locations and summer for the deficit form of the disease
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFHJHJ') Brown 2011
          | )
        | . The incidence of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  and related disorders is higher for children growing up in an urban environment
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHDAHJA') March et al. 2008
          | )
        |  and for some minority ethnic groups
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHIJHFJ') Bourque et al. 2011
          | )
        | .
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2414633')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2414372') Genetic and physiological
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | There is a strong contribution for genetic factors in determining risk for
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHGBICG') Sullivan et al. 2003
          | )
        | , although most individuals who have been diagnosed with
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  have no family history of psychosis
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJAJAH') Mortensen et al. 2010
          | )
        | . Liability is conferred by a spectrum of risk alleles, common and rare, with each allele contributing only a small fraction to the total population variance
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCGHHA') Owen et al. 2010
          | )
        | . The risk alleles identified to date are also associated with other mental disorders, including
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName bipolar disorder
        | , depression, and
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName autism spectrum disorder
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCGHHA') Owen et al. 2010
          | )
        | .
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Pregnancy and birth complications with hypoxia and greater paternal age
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHGECHE') Miller et al. 2011
          | )
        |  are associated with a higher risk of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  for the developing fetus. In addition, other prenatal and perinatal adversities, including stress, infection, malnutrition, maternal diabetes, and other medical conditions, have been linked with
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFHJHJ') Brown 2011
          | )
        | . However, the vast majority of offspring with these risk factors do not develop schizophrenia.
  .APP-CultureRelatedDiagnosticIssues(id='x92567.2414806')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2414398') Culture-Related Diagnostic Issues
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Cultural and socioeconomic factors must be considered, particularly when the individual and the clinician do not share the same cultural and socioeconomic background. Ideas that appear to be delusional in one culture (e.g., witchcraft) may be commonly held in another. In some cultures, visual or auditory hallucinations with a religious content (e.g., hearing God’s voice) are a normal part of religious experience. In addition, the assessment of disorganized speech may be made difficult by linguistic variation in narrative styles across cultures. The assessment of affect requires sensitivity to differences in styles of emotional expression, eye contact, and body language, which vary across cultures. If the assessment is conducted in a language that is different from the individual’s primary language, care must be taken to ensure that alogia is not related to linguistic barriers. In certain cultures, distress may take the form of hallucinations or pseudo-hallucinations and overvalued ideas that may present clinically similar to true psychosis but are normative to the patient’s subgroup.
  .APP-GenderRelatedDiagnosticIssues(id='x92567.2414810')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2414413') Gender-Related Diagnostic Issues
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | A number of features distinguish the clinical expression of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  in females and males. The general incidence of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  tends to be slightly lower in females, particularly among treated cases. The age at onset is later in females, with a second mid-life peak
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFHJBJ') Abel et al. 2010
        | )
      |  as described earlier (see the section “Development and Course” for this disorder). Symptoms tend to be more affect-laden among females, and there are more psychotic symptoms, as well as a greater propensity for psychotic symptoms to worsen in later life
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFHJBJ') Abel et al. 2010
        | )
      | . Other symptom differences include less frequent negative symptoms and disorganization. Finally, social functioning tends to remain better preserved in females. There are, however, frequent exceptions to these general caveats.
  .APP-SuicideRisk(id='x92567.2414819')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2414428') Suicide Risk
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Approximately 5%–6% of individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  die by suicide, about 20% attempt suicide on one or more occasions, and many more have significant suicidal ideation
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHBGDCF') Hawton et al. 2005
        | )
      | . Suicidal behavior is sometimes in response to command hallucinations to harm oneself or others. Suicide risk remains high over the whole lifespan for males and females, although it may be especially high for younger males with comorbid substance use. Other risk factors include having depressive symptoms or feelings of hopelessness and being unemployed, and the risk is higher, also, in the period after a psychotic episode or hospital discharge
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHBGDCF') Hawton et al. 2005
        | )
      | .
  .APP-FunctionalConsequences(id='x92567.2414827')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2414447') Functional Consequences of Schizophrenia
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Schizophrenia is associated with significant social and occupational dysfunction. Making educational progress and maintaining employment are frequently impaired by avolition or other disorder manifestations, even when the cognitive skills are sufficient for the tasks at hand. Most individuals are employed at a lower level than their parents, and most, particularly men, do not marry or have limited social contacts outside of their family.
  .APP-DifferentialDiagnosis(id='x92567.2414831')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2414457') Differential Diagnosis
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2414648')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2414462') Major depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic or catatonic features
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | The distinction between schizophrenia and major depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features or with catatonia depends on the temporal relationship between the mood disturbance and the psychosis, and on the severity of the depressive or manic symptoms. If delusions or hallucinations occur exclusively during a major depressive or manic episode, the diagnosis is depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2414657')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2414477') Schizoaffective disorder
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | A diagnosis of schizoaffective disorder requires that a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName major depressive or manic episode
        |  occur concurrently with the active-phase symptoms and that the mood symptoms be present for a majority of the total duration of the active periods.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2414664')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2414490') Schizophreniform disorder and brief psychotic disorder
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | These disorders are of shorter duration than schizophrenia as specified in Criterion C, which requires 6 months of symptoms. In
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophreniform disorder,
        |  the disturbance is present less than 6 months, and in
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder
        | , symptoms are present at least 1 day but less than 1 month.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2414671')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2414503') Delusional disorder
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Delusional disorder can be distinguished from schizophrenia by the absence of the other symptoms characteristic of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  (e.g., delusions, prominent auditory or visual hallucinations, disorganized speech, grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior, negative symptoms).
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2414677')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2414515') Schizotypal personality disorder
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName Schizotypal personality disorder
        |  may be distinguished from
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  by subthreshold symptoms that are associated with persistent personality features.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2414683')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2414526') Obsessive-compulsive disorder and body dysmorphic disorder
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Individuals with obsessive-compulsive disorder and body dysmorphic disorder may present with poor or absent insight, and the preoccupations may reach delusional proportions. But these disorders are distinguished from
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  by their prominent obsessions, compulsions, preoccupations with appearance or body odor, hoarding, or body-focused repetitive behaviors.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2414689')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2414538') Posttraumatic stress disorder
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Posttraumatic stress disorder may include flashbacks that have a hallucinatory quality, and hypervigilance may reach paranoid proportions. But a traumatic event and characteristic symptom features relating to reliving or reacting to the event are required to make the diagnosis.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2414693')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2414548') Autism spectrum disorder or communication disorders
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | These disorders may also have symptoms resembling a psychotic episode but are distinguished by their respective deficits in social interaction with repetitive and restricted behaviors and other cognitive and communication deficits. An individual with
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName autism spectrum disorder
        |  or
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName communication disorder
        |  must have symptoms that meet full criteria for schizophrenia, with prominent hallucinations or delusions for at least 1 month, in order to be diagnosed with
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  as a comorbid condition.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2414701')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2414562') Other mental disorders associated with a psychotic episode
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | The diagnosis of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  is made only when the psychotic episode is persistent and not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance or another medical condition. Individuals with a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delirium
        |  or
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName major or minor neurocognitive disorder
        |  may present with psychotic symptoms, but these would have a temporal relationship to the onset of cognitive changes consistent with those disorders. Individuals with
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder
        |  may present with symptoms characteristic of Criterion A for
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        | , but the
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder
        |  can usually be distinguished by the chronological relationship of substance use to the onset and remission of the psychosis in the absence of substance use.
  .APP-Comorbidity(id='x92567.2414863')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2414579') Comorbidity
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Rates of comorbidity with substance-related disorders are high in s
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName chizophrenia
      | . Over half of individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  have tobacco use disorder and smoke cigarettes regularly
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFJHCI') de Leon and Diaz 2005
        | )
      | . Comorbidity with anxiety disorders is increasingly recognized in
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | . Rates of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName obsessive-compulsive disorder
      |  and
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName panic disorder
      |  are elevated in individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  compared with the general population.
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName Schizotypal or paranoid personality disorder
      |  may sometimes precede the onset of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | .
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Life expectancy is reduced in individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  because of associated medical conditions. Weight gain, diabetes, metabolic syndrome, and cardiovascular and pulmonary disease are more common in
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  than in the general population
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJGIFC') Hennekens 2007
        | )
      | . Poor engagement in health maintenance behaviors (e.g., cancer screening, exercise) increases the risk of chronic disease, but other disorder factors, including medications, lifestyle, cigarette smoking, and diet, may also play a role. A shared vulnerability for psychosis and medical disorders may explain some of the medical comorbidity of schizophrenia.
