    h2.sectionHeader Schizoaffective Disorder
  .APP-CriteriaSet(id='x92567.2409836')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2426895') Diagnostic Criteria
    span.APP-ICDCode.rightICDCode
      span.APP-ICD9Code
    ol.APP-List.APP-AlphaUpper(start='1')
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | An uninterrupted period of illness during which there is a major mood episode (major depressive or manic) concurrent with Criterion A of schizophrenia.
        .APP-CriteriaSetNote
          ul.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-UnNumberedUnindented
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
                b Note:
                |  The major depressive episode must include Criterion A1: Depressed mood.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | Delusions or hallucinations for 2 or more weeks in the absence of a major mood episode (depressive or manic) during the lifetime duration of the illness.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | Symptoms that meet criteria for a major mood episode are present for the majority of the total duration of the active and residual portions of the illness.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | The disturbance is not attributable to the effects of a substance (e.g., a drug of abuse, a medication) or another medical condition.
    .APP-NoteIntro
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
      |  whether:
    ul.APP-List.APP-UnNumbered
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          span.APP-ICDCode.inlineICDCode
            span.APP-ICD9Code 295.70
            span.ICD10Code (F25.0)
          b Bipolar type:
          |  This subtype applies if a manic episode is part of the presentation. Major depressive episodes may also occur.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          span.APP-ICDCode.inlineICDCode
            span.APP-ICD9Code 295.70
            span.ICD10Code (F25.1)
          b Depressive type:
          |  This subtype applies if only major depressive episodes are part of the presentation.
    .APP-NoteIntro
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
      |  if:
    ul.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-UnNumbered
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          b With catatonia
          |  (refer to the criteria for catatonia associated with another mental disorder, pp. 119–120, for definition).
        .APP-CodeNote
          ul.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-UnNumberedIndented
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
                b Coding note:
                |  Use additional code 293.89 (F06.1) catatonia associated with schizoaffective disorder to indicate the presence of the comorbid catatonia.
    .APP-CourseSpecifierNote
      .APP-NoteIntro
        span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
        |  if:
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | The following course specifiers are only to be used after a 1-year duration of the disorder and if they are not in contradiction to the diagnostic course criteria.
      ul.APP-List.APP-UnNumbered
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b First episode, currently in acute episode:
            |  First manifestation of the disorder meeting the defining diagnostic symptom and time criteria. An
            span.APP-EmphasisItalic acute episode
            |  is a time period in which the symptom criteria are fulfilled.
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b First episode, currently in partial remission:
            span.APP-EmphasisItalic Partial remission
            |  is a time period during which an improvement after a previous episode is maintained and in which the defining criteria of the disorder are only partially fulfilled.
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b First episode, currently in full remission:
            span.APP-EmphasisItalic Full remission
            |  is a period of time after a previous episode during which no disorder-specific symptoms are present.
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Multiple episodes, currently in acute episode:
            |  Multiple episodes may be determined after a minimum of two episodes (i.e., after a first episode, a remission and a minimum of one relapse).
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Multiple episodes, currently in partial remission
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Multiple episodes, currently in full remission
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Continuous:
            |  Symptoms fulfilling the diagnostic symptom criteria of the disorder are remaining for the majority of the illness course, with subthreshold symptom periods being very brief relative to the overall course.
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Unspecified
    .APP-SeveritySpecifierNote
      .APP-NoteIntro
        span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
        |  current severity:
      ul.APP-List.APP-UnNumbered
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            | Severity is rated by a quantitative assessment of the primary symptoms of psychosis, including delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, abnormal psychomotor behavior, and negative symptoms. Each of these symptoms may be rated for its current severity (most severe in the last 7 days) on a 5-point scale ranging from 0 (not present) to 4 (present and severe). (See Clinician-Rated Dimensions of Psychosis Symptom Severity in the chapter “Assessment Measures.”)
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            b Note:
            |  Diagnosis of schizoaffective disorder can be made without using this severity specifier.
  .APP-DisorderNote
    b Note:
    |  For additional information on Development and Course (age-related factors), Risk and Prognostic Factors (environmental risk factors), Culture-Related Diagnostic Issues, and Gender-Related Diagnostic Issues, see the corresponding sections in schizophrenia, bipolar I and II disorders, and major depressive disorder in their respective chapters.
  .APP-DiagnosticFeatures(id='x92567.2413092')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2412721') Diagnostic Features
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      |  is based on the assessment of an uninterrupted period of illness during which the individual continues to display active or residual symptoms of psychotic illness. The diagnosis is usually, but not necessarily, made during the period of psychotic illness. At some time during the period, Criterion A for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  has to be met. Criteria B (social dysfunction) and F (exclusion of autism spectrum disorder or other communication disorder of childhood onset) for schizophrenia do not have to be met. In addition to meeting Criterion A for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | , there is a
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName major mood episode (major depressive
      |  or
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName manic)
      |  (Criterion A for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | ). Because loss of interest or pleasure is common in
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | , to meet Criterion A for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | , the
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName major depressive episode
      |  must include pervasive depressed mood (i.e., the presence of markedly diminished interest or pleasure is not sufficient). Episodes of depression or mania are present for the majority of the total duration of the illness (i.e., after Criterion A has been met) (Criterion C for schizoaffective disorder). To separate
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      |  from a
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features
      | , delusions or hallucinations must be present for at least 2 weeks in the absence of a major mood episode (depressive or manic) at some point during the lifetime duration of the illness (Criterion B for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | ). The symptoms must not be attributable to the effects of a substance or another medical condition (Criterion D for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | ).
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Criterion C for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      |  specifies that mood symptoms meeting criteria for a major mood episode must be present for the majority of the total duration of the active and residual portion of the illness. Criterion C requires the assessment of mood symptoms for the entire course of a psychotic illness, which differs from the criterion in DSM-IV, which required only an assessment of the current period of illness. If the mood symptoms are present for only a relatively brief period, the diagnosis is
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | , not
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | . When deciding whether an individual’s presentation meets Criterion C, the clinician should review the total duration of psychotic illness (i.e., both active and residual symptoms) and determine when significant mood symptoms (untreated or in need of treatment with antidepressant and/or mood-stabilizing medication) accompanied the psychotic symptoms. This determination requires sufficient historical information and clinical judgment. For example, an individual with a 4-year history of active and residual symptoms of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  develops
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName depressive and manic episodes
      |  that, taken together, do not occupy more than 1 year during the 4-year history of psychotic illness. This presentation would not meet Criterion C.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | In addition to the five symptom domain areas identified in the diagnostic criteria, the assessment of cognition, depression, and mania symptom domains is vital for making critically important distinctions between the various schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders.
  .APP-AssociatedFeatures(id='x92567.2413119')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2412764') Associated Features Supporting Diagnosis
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Occupational functioning is frequently impaired, but this is not a defining criterion (in contrast to
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | ). Restricted social contact and difficulties with self-care are associated with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | , but negative symptoms may be less severe and less persistent than those seen in
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHDIIJA') Cheniaux et al. 2008
        | )
      | . Anosognosia (i.e., poor insight) is also common in
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | , but the deficits in insight may be less severe and pervasive than those in
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHHDJEI') Drake 2008
        | )
      | . Individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      |  may be at increased risk for later developing episodes of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName major depressive disorder
      |  or
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName bipolar disorder
      |  if mood symptoms continue following the remission of symptoms meeting Criterion A for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | . There may be associated
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName alcohol and other substance-related disorders
      | .
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | There are no tests or biological measures that can assist in making the diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | . Whether
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      |  differs from
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  with regard to associated features such as structural or functional brain abnormalities, cognitive deficits, or genetic risk factors is not clear
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHBIHCE') Bora et al.
          |  2009
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHDIIJA') Cheniaux et al. 2008
        | )
      | .
  h3.Head(id='x92567.2412796') Prevalence
  p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName Schizoaffective disorder
    |  appears to be about one-third as common as
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
    | . Lifetime prevalence of
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
    |  is estimated to be 0.3%
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCHHEI') Perälä et al.
        |  2007
      | )
    | . The incidence of
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
    |  is higher in females than in males, mainly due to an increased incidence of the depressive type among females
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHHGIIA') Malhi et al. 2008
      | )
    | .
  .APP-DevelopmentAndCourse(id='x92567.2413150')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2412811') Development and Course
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The typical age at onset of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      |  is early adulthood, although onset can occur anywhere from adolescence to late in life. A significant number of individuals diagnosed with another psychotic illness initially will receive the diagnosis
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      |  later when the pattern of mood episodes has become more apparent
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHDFBFD') Bromet et al.
          |  2011
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHIGDIA') Salvatore et al. 2009
        | )
      | . With the current diagnostic Criterion C, it is expected that the diagnosis for some individuals will convert from schizoaffective disorder to another disorder as mood symptoms become less prominent. The prognosis for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      |  is somewhat better than the prognosis for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  but worse than the prognosis for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName mood disorders
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHEGBGD') Harrow et al. 2000
        | )
      | .
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName Schizoaffective disorder
      |  may occur in a variety of temporal patterns. The following is a typical pattern: An individual may have pronounced auditory hallucinations and persecutory delusions for 2 months before the onset of a prominent
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName major depressive episode
      | . The psychotic symptoms and the full
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName major depressive episode
      |  are then present for 3 months. Then, the individual recovers completely from the
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName major depressive episode
      | , but the psychotic symptoms persist for another month before they too disappear. During this period of illness, the individual’s symptoms concurrently met criteria for a
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName major depressive episode
      |  and Criterion A for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | , and during this same period of illness, auditory hallucinations and delusions were present both before and after the depressive phase. The total period of illness lasted for about 6 months, with psychotic symptoms alone present during the initial 2 months, both depressive and psychotic symptoms present during the next 3 months, and psychotic symptoms alone present during the last month. In this instance, the duration of the depressive episode was not brief relative to the total duration of the psychotic disturbance, and thus the presentation qualifies for a diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | .
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The expression of psychotic symptoms across the lifespan is variable. Depressive or manic symptoms can occur before the onset of psychosis, during acute psychotic episodes, during residual periods, and after cessation of psychosis. For example, an individual might present with prominent mood symptoms during the prodromal stage of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | . This pattern is not necessarily indicative of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | , since it is the co-occurrence of psychotic and mood symptoms that is diagnostic. For an individual with symptoms that clearly meet
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName the criteria for schizoaffective disorder
      |  but who on further follow-up only presents with residual psychotic symptoms (such as subthreshold psychosis and/or prominent negative symptoms), the diagnosis may be changed to
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | , as the total proportion of psychotic illness compared with mood symptoms becomes more prominent.
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName Schizoaffective disorder, bipolar type,
      |  may be more common in young adults, whereas
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder, depressive type,
      |  may be more common in older adults.
  .APP-RiskAndPrognosticFactors(id='x92567.2413176')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2412852') Risk and Prognostic Factors
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2413041')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2412857') Genetic and physiological
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Among individuals with schizophrenia, there may be an increased risk for
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
        |  in first-degree relatives
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHICGFF') Potash 2006
          | )
        | . The risk for schizoaffective disorder may be increased among individuals who have a first-degree relative with
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        | ,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName bipolar disorder
        | , or
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHIIFGF') Laursen et al. 2005
          | )
        | .
  .APP-CultureRelatedDiagnosticIssues(id='x92567.2413187')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2412874') Culture-Related Diagnostic Issues
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Cultural and socioeconomic factors must be considered, particularly when the individual and the clinician do not share the same cultural and economic background. Ideas that appear to be delusional in one culture (e.g., witchcraft) may be commonly held in another. There is also some evidence in the literature for the overdiagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  compared with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      |  in African American and Hispanic populations, so care must be taken to ensure a culturally appropriate evaluation that includes both psychotic and affective symptoms
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHDGEIE') Anglin and Malaspina 2008
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJIEFC') Blow et al. 2004
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHHBAEB') Strakowski et al. 1996
        | )
      | .
  .APP-SuicideRisk(id='x92567.2413204')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2412927') Suicide Risk
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The lifetime risk of suicide for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  and
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      |  is 5%, and the presence of depressive symptoms is correlated with a higher risk for suicide
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHCGAJG') Hor and Taylor 2010
        | )
      | . There is evidence that suicide rates are higher in North American populations than in European, Eastern European, South American, and Indian populations of individuals with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      |  or
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFGFHA') Altamura et al. 2007
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHICIFF') Bhatia et al. 2006
        | )
      | .
  .APP-FunctionalConsequences(id='x92567.2413215')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2412913') Functional Consequences of Schizoaffective Disorder
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName Schizoaffective disorder
      |  is associated with social and occupational dysfunction
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHBDGJG') Heckers 2009
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHEJBCC') Lysaker and Davis 2004
        | )
      | , but dysfunction is not a diagnostic criterion (as it is for
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
      | ), and there is substantial variability between individuals diagnosed with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      | .
  .APP-DifferentialDiagnosis(id='x92567.2413224')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2412944') Differential Diagnosis
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2413052')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2412949') Other mental disorders and medical conditions
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | A wide variety of psychiatric and medical conditions can manifest with psychotic and mood symptoms that must be considered in the differential diagnosis of schizoaffective disorder. These include psychotic disorder due to another medical condition; delirium; major neurocognitive disorder; substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder or neurocognitive disorder; bipolar disorders with psychotic features; major depressive disorder with psychotic features; depressive or bipolar disorders with catatonic features; schizotypal, schizoid, or paranoid personality disorder; brief psychotic disorder; schizophreniform disorder; schizophrenia; delusional disorder; and other specified and unspecified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders. Medical conditions and substance use can present with a combination of psychotic and mood symptoms, and thus psychotic disorder due to another medical condition needs to be excluded. Distinguishing schizoaffective disorder from schizophrenia and from depressive and bipolar disorders with psychotic features is often difficult. Criterion C is designed to separate schizoaffective disorder from schizophrenia, and Criterion B is designed to distinguish schizoaffective disorder from a depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features. More specifically, schizoaffective disorder can be distinguished from a depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features due to the presence of prominent delusions and/or hallucinations for at least 2 weeks in the absence of a major mood episode. In contrast, in depressive or bipolar disorders with psychotic features, the psychotic features primarily occur during the mood episode(s). Because the relative proportion of mood to psychotic symptoms may change over time, the appropriate diagnosis may change from and to schizoaffective disorder (e.g., a diagnosis of schizoaffective disorder for a severe and prominent major depressive episode lasting 3 months during the first 6 months of a persistent psychotic illness would be changed to schizophrenia if active psychotic or prominent residual symptoms persist over several years without a recurrence of another mood episode).
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2413070')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2412978') Psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Other medical conditions and substance use can manifest with a combination of psychotic and mood symptoms, and thus
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
        |  needs to be excluded.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2413076')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2412990') Schizophrenia, bipolar, and depressive disorders
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Distinguishing
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
        |  from
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  and from
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName depressive and bipolar disorders with psychotic features
        |  is often difficult. Criterion C is designed to separate
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
        |  from
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        | , and Criterion B is designed to distinguish
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
        |  from a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features
        | . More specifically,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
        |  can be distinguished from a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features
        |  based on the presence of prominent delusions and/or hallucinations for at least 2 weeks in the absence of a major mood episode. In contrast, in
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName depressive or bipolar disorder with psychotic features
        | , the psychotic features primarily occur during the mood episode(s). Because the relative proportion of mood to psychotic symptoms may change over time, the appropriate diagnosis may change from and to
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder.
        |  (For example, a diagnosis of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
        |  for a severe and prominent
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName major depressive episode
        |  lasting 3 months during the first 6 months of a chronic psychotic illness would be changed to
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  if active psychotic or prominent residual symptoms persist over several years without a recurrence of another mood episode.)
  .APP-Comorbidity(id='x92567.2427963')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2413012') Comorbidity
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Many individuals diagnosed with
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
      |  are also diagnosed with other mental disorders, especially
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance use disorders
      |  and
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName anxiety disorders
      | . Similarly, the incidence of medical conditions is increased above base rate for the general population and leads to decreased life expectancy
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHHGECH') Chang et al. 2011
        | )
      | .
