    h2.sectionHeader Psychotic Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition
  .APP-CriteriaSet(id='x92567.2601837')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2601721') Diagnostic Criteria
    span.APP-ICDCode.inlineICDCode
      span.APP-ICD9Code
    ol.APP-List.APP-AlphaUpper(start='1')
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Prominent hallucinations or delusions.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | There is evidence from the history, physical examination, or laboratory findings that the disturbance is the direct pathophysiological consequence of another medical condition.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize The disturbance is not better explained by another mental disorder.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize The disturbance does not occur exclusively during the course of a delirium.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          | The disturbance causes clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning.
    .APP-NoteIntro
      span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
      |  whether:
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Code based on predominant symptom:
    ul.APP-List.APP-UnNumbered
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          span.APP-ICDCode.inlineICDCode
            span.APP-ICD9Code 293.81
            span.ICD10Code (F06.2)
          b With delusions:
          |  If delusions are the predominant symptom.
      li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          span.APP-ICDCode.inlineICDCode
            span.APP-ICD9Code 293.82
            span.ICD10Code (F06.0)
          b With hallucinations:
          |  If hallucinations are the predominant symptom.
    .APP-CodeNote
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        b Coding note:
        |  Include the name of the other medical condition in the name of the mental disorder (e.g., 293.81 [F06.2] psychotic disorder due to malignant lung neoplasm, with delusions). The other medical condition should be coded and listed separately immediately before the psychotic disorder due to the medical condition (e.g., 162.9 [C34.90] malignant lung neoplasm; 293.81 [F06.2] psychotic disorder due to malignant lung neoplasm, with delusions).
    .APP-SeveritySpecifierNote
      .APP-NoteIntro
        span.APP-EmphasisItalic Specify
        |  current severity:
      ul.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-UnNumbered
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            | Severity is rated by a quantitative assessment of the primary symptoms of psychosis, including delusions, hallucinations, abnormal psychomotor behavior, and negative symptoms. Each of these symptoms may be rated for its current severity (most severe in the last 7 days) on a 5-point scale ranging from 0 (not present) to 4 (present and severe). (See Clinician-Rated Dimensions of Psychosis Symptom Severity in the chapter “Assessment Measures.”)
          .APP-CriteriaSetNote
            ul.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-UnNumberedUnindented
              li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
                p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
                  b Note:
                  |  Diagnosis of
                  span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
                  |  can be made without using this severity specifier.
  .APP-SubtypesAndSpecifiers(id='x92567.2603018')
    .APP-Specifiers(id='x92567.2603014')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2602744') Specifiers
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | In addition to the symptom domain areas identified in the diagnostic criteria, the assessment of cognition, depression, and mania symptom domains is vital for making critically important distinctions between the various schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders.
  .APP-DiagnosticFeatures(id='x92567.2603022')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2602754') Diagnostic Features
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The essential features of psychotic disorder due to another medical condition are prominent delusions or hallucinations that are judged to be attributable to the physiological effects of another medical condition and are not better explained by another mental disorder (e.g., the symptoms are not a psychologically mediated response to a severe medical condition, in which case a diagnosis of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName brief psychotic disorder, with marked stressor
      | , would be appropriate).
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Hallucinations can occur in any sensory modality (i.e., visual, olfactory, gustatory, tactile, or auditory), but certain etiological factors are likely to evoke specific hallucinatory phenomena. Olfactory hallucinations are suggestive of temporal lobe epilepsy. Hallucinations may vary from simple and unformed to highly complex and organized, depending on etiological and environmental factors.
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName Psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
      |  is generally not diagnosed if the individual maintains reality testing for the hallucinations and appreciates that they result from the medical condition. Delusions may have a variety of themes, including somatic, grandiose, religious, and, most commonly, persecutory. On the whole, however, associations between delusions and particular medical conditions appear to be less specific than is the case for hallucinations.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | In determining whether the psychotic disturbance is attributable to another medical condition, the presence of a medical condition must be identified and considered to be the etiology of the psychosis through a physiological mechanism. Although there are no infallible guidelines for determining whether the relationship between the psychotic disturbance and the medical condition is etiological, several considerations provide some guidance. One consideration is the presence of a temporal association between the onset, exacerbation, or remission of the medical condition and that of the psychotic disturbance. A second consideration is the presence of features that are atypical for a
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder
      |  (e.g., atypical age at onset or presence of visual or olfactory hallucinations). The disturbance must also be distinguished from a
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder
      |  or another mental disorder (e.g., an adjustment disorder).
  .APP-AssociatedFeatures(id='x92567.2603033')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2602781') Associated Features Supporting Diagnosis
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The temporal association of the onset or exacerbation of the medical condition offers the greatest diagnostic certainty that the delusions or hallucinations are attributable to a medical condition. Additional factors may include concomitant treatments for the underlying medical condition that confer a risk for psychosis independently, such as steroid treatment for autoimmune disorders.
  h3.Head(id='x92567.2602791') Prevalence
  p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
    | Prevalence rates for
    span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
    |  are difficult to estimate given the wide variety of underlying medical etiologies. Lifetime prevalence has been estimated to range from 0.21% to 0.54%
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHDCEGF') Bogren et al. 2009
      | ;
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHIDHEE') Perälä
        |  et al. 2007
      | )
    | . When the prevalence findings are stratified by age group, individuals older than 65 years have a significantly greater prevalence of 0.74% compared with those in younger age groups
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHIDHEE') Perälä
        |  et al. 2007
      | )
    | . Rates of psychosis also vary according to the underlying medical condition; conditions most commonly associated with psychosis include untreated endocrine and metabolic disorders, autoimmune disorders (e.g., systemic lupus erythematosus,
    span.APP-EmphasisItalic N
    | -methyl-
    span.APP-EmphasisSmCap d
    | -aspartate (NMDA) receptor autoimmune encephalitis), or temporal lobe epilepsy. Psychosis due to epilepsy has been further differentiated into ictal, postictal, and interictal psychosis. The most common of these is postictal psychosis, observed in 2%–7.8% of epilepsy patients
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHGDCBC') Nadkarni et al. 2007
      | )
    | . Among older individuals, there may be a higher prevalence of the disorder in females
    span.APP-CiteGroup
      | (
      span.APP-Cite
        a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHIDHEE') Perälä
        |  et al. 2007
      | )
    | , although additional gender-related features are not clear and vary considerably with the gender distributions of the underlying medical conditions.
  .APP-DevelopmentAndCourse(id='x92567.2603049')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2602809') Development and Course
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName Psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
      |  may be a single transient state or it may be recurrent, cycling with exacerbations and remissions of the underlying medical condition. Although treatment of the underlying medical condition often results in a resolution of the psychosis, this is not always the case, and psychotic symptoms may persist long after the medical event (e.g.,
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to focal brain injury
      | ). In the context of chronic conditions such as multiple sclerosis or chronic interictal psychosis of epilepsy, the psychosis may assume a long-term course.
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The expression of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
      |  does not differ substantially in phenomenology depending on age at occurrence. However, older age groups have a higher prevalence of the disorder, which is most likely due to the increasing medical burden associated with advanced age and the cumulative effects of deleterious exposures and age-related processes (e.g., atherosclerosis). The nature of the underlying medical conditions is likely to change across the lifespan, with younger age groups more affected by epilepsy, head trauma, autoimmune, and neoplastic diseases of early to mid-life, and older age groups more affected by stroke disease, anoxic events, and multiple system comorbidities. Underlying factors with increasing age, such as preexisting cognitive impairment as well as vision and hearing impairments, may incur a greater risk for psychosis, possibly by serving to lower the threshold for experiencing psychosis.
  .APP-RiskAndPrognosticFactors(id='x92567.2603057')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2602827') Risk and Prognostic Factors
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2602971')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2602832') Course modifiers
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Identification and treatment of the underlying medical condition has the greatest impact on course, although preexisting central nervous system injury may confer a worse course outcome (e.g., head trauma, cerebrovascular disease).
  .APP-DiagnosticMarkers(id='x92567.2603067')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2602854') Diagnostic Markers
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | The diagnosis of psychotic disorder due to another medical condition depends on the clinical condition of each individual, and the diagnostic tests will vary according to that condition. A variety of medical conditions may cause psychotic symptoms. These include neurological conditions (e.g., neoplasms, cerebrovascular disease, Huntington's disease, multiple sclerosis, epilepsy, auditory or visual nerve injury or impairment, deafness, migraine, central nervous system infections), endocrine conditions (e.g., hyper- and hypothyroidism, hyper- and hypoparathyroidism, hyper- and hypoadrenocorticism), metabolic conditions (e.g., hypoxia, hypercarbia, hypoglycemia), fluid or electrolyte imbalances, hepatic or renal diseases, and autoimmune disorders with central nervous system involvement (e.g., systemic lupus erythematosus). The associated physical examination findings, laboratory findings, and patterns of prevalence or onset reflect the etiological medical condition.
  .APP-SuicideRisk(id='x92567.2603071')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2602864') Suicide Risk
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Suicide risk in the context of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
      |  is not clearly delineated, although certain conditions such as epilepsy and multiple sclerosis are associated with increased rates of suicide
      span.APP-CiteGroup
        | (
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHHFBJB') Bagary 2011
        | ;
        span.APP-Cite
          a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFHEFF') Brønnum-Hansen et al. 2005
        | )
      | , which may be further increased in the presence of psychosis.
  .APP-FunctionalConsequences(id='x92567.2603079')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2602878') Functional Consequences of Psychotic Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition
    p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
      | Functional disability is typically severe in the context of
      span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
      |  but will vary considerably by the type of condition and likely improve with successful resolution of the condition.
  .APP-DifferentialDiagnosis(id='x92567.2603085')
    h3.Head(id='x92567.2602890') Differential Diagnosis
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2602975')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2602895') Delirium
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Hallucinations and delusions commonly occur in the context of a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delirium
        | ; however, a separate diagnosis of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
        |  is not given if the disturbance occurs exclusively during the course of a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delirium
        | . Delusions in the context of a major or mild neurocognitive disorder would be diagnosed as major or mild neurocognitive disorder, with behavioral disturbance.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2602983')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2604891') Substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | If there is evidence of recent or prolonged substance use (including medications with psychoactive effects), withdrawal from a substance, or exposure to a toxin (e.g.,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName LSD [lysergic acid diethylamide] intoxication
        | ,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName alcohol withdrawal
        | ), a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder
        |  should be considered. Symptoms that occur during or shortly after (i.e., within 4 weeks) of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance intoxication or withdrawal
        |  or after medication use may be especially indicative of a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance-induced psychotic disorder
        | , depending on the character, duration, or amount of the substance used. If the clinician has ascertained that the disturbance is due to both a medical condition and substance use, both diagnoses (i.e.,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
        |  and
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder
        | ) can be given.
    section.APP-Section(id='x92567.2602995')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2602927') Psychotic disorder
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName Psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
        |  must be distinguished from a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder
        |  (e.g.,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        | ,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
        | ,
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizoaffective disorder
        | ) or a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName depressive or bipolar disorder, with psychotic features
        | . In
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorders
        |  and in
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName depressive or bipolar disorders, with psychotic features
        | , no specific and direct causative physiological mechanisms associated with a medical condition can be demonstrated. Late age at onset and the absence of a personal or family history of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  or
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName delusional disorder
        |  suggest the need for a thorough assessment to rule out the diagnosis of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
        | . Auditory hallucinations that involve voices speaking complex sentences are more characteristic of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName schizophrenia
        |  than of
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to a medical condition
        | . Other types of hallucinations (e.g., visual, olfactory) commonly signal a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName psychotic disorder due to another medical condition
        |  or a
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder
        | .
