      h2.sectionHeader Catatonia Associated With Another Mental Disorder (Catatonia Specifier)
    .APP-CriteriaSet(id='x92567.2411740')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2446972')
      span.APP-ICDCode.rightICDCode
        span.APP-ICD9Code 293.89
        span.ICD10Code (F06.1)
      ol.APP-ListSingleItem.APP-AlphaUpper(start='1')
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize The clinical picture is dominated by three (or more) of the following symptoms: 
          ol.APP-List.APP-NumberedGE10(start='1')
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Stupor (i.e., no psychomotor activity; not actively relating to environment).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Catalepsy (i.e., passive induction of a posture held against gravity).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Waxy flexibility (i.e., slight, even resistance to positioning by examiner).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Mutism (i.e., no, or very little, verbal response [exclude if known aphasia]).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
                | Negativism (i.e., opposition or no response to instructions or external stimuli).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
                | Posturing (i.e., spontaneous and active maintenance of a posture against gravity).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Mannerism (i.e., odd, circumstantial caricature of normal actions).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Stereotypy (i.e., repetitive, abnormally frequent, non-goal-directed movements).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Agitation, not influenced by external stimuli.
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Grimacing.
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Echolalia (i.e., mimicking another’s speech).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Echopraxia (i.e., mimicking another’s movements).
      .APP-CodeNote
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          b Coding note:
          |  Indicate the name of the associated mental disorder when recording the name of the condition (i.e., 293.89 [F06.1] catatonia associated with major depressive disorder). Code first the associated mental disorder (e.g., neurodevelopmental disorder, brief psychotic disorder, schizophreniform disorder, schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, bipolar disorder, major depressive disorder, or other mental disorder) (e.g., 295.70 [F25.1] schizoaffective disorder, depressive type; 293.89 [F06.1] catatonia associated with schizoaffective disorder).
    .APP-DiagnosticFeatures(id='x92567.2418118')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2418071') Diagnostic Features
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | Catatonia associated with another mental disorder (catatonia specifier) may be used when criteria are met for catatonia during the course of a neurodevelopmental, psychotic, bipolar, depressive, or other mental disorder. The catatonia specifier is appropriate when the clinical picture is characterized by marked psychomotor disturbance and involves at least three of the 12 diagnostic features listed in Criterion A. Catatonia is typically diagnosed in an inpatient setting and occurs in up to 35% of individuals with s
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName chizophrenia
        | , but the majority of catatonia cases involve individuals with depressive or bipolar disorders
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFDBAA') Kruger and Bräunig 2000
          | ;
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHICDJH') Kruger et al. 2003
          | ;
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHHCJFI') Stompe et al. 2002
          | ;
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHGHJBE') Ungvari et al. 2005
          | ;
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHFIGCD') Wender et al. 2008
          | )
        | . Before the catatonia specifier is used in neurodevelopmental, psychotic, bipolar, depressive, or other mental disorders, a wide variety of other medical conditions need to be ruled out; these conditions include, but are not limited to, medical conditions due to infectious, metabolic, or neurological conditions (see “Catatonic Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition”). Catatonia can also be a side effect of a medication (see the chapter “Medication-Induced Movement Disorders and Other Adverse Effects of Medication”). Because of the seriousness of the complications, particular attention should be paid to the possibility that the catatonia is attributable to 333.92 (G21.0) neuroleptic malignant syndrome.
