      h2.sectionHeader Catatonic Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition
    .APP-CriteriaSet(id='x92567.2433757')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2433760') Diagnostic Criteria
      span.APP-ICDCode.rightICDCode
        span.APP-ICD9Code 293.89
        span.ICD10Code (F06.1)
      ol.APP-List.APP-AlphaUpper(start='1')
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize The clinical picture is dominated by three (or more) of the following symptoms: 
          ol.APP-List.APP-NumberedGE10(start='1')
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Stupor (i.e., no psychomotor activity; not actively relating to environment).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Catalepsy (i.e., passive induction of a posture held against gravity).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Waxy flexibility (i.e., slight, even resistance to positioning by examiner).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
                | Mutism (i.e., no, or very little, verbal response [
                b Note:
                |  not applicable if there is an established aphasia]).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
                | Negativism (i.e., opposition or no response to instructions or external stimuli).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
                | Posturing (i.e., spontaneous and active maintenance of a posture against gravity).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Mannerism (i.e., odd, circumstantial caricature of normal actions).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Stereotypy (i.e., repetitive, abnormally frequent, non-goal-directed movements).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Agitation, not influenced by external stimuli.
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Grimacing.
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Echolalia (i.e., mimicking another’s speech).
            li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
              p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize Echopraxia (i.e., mimicking another’s movements).
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            | There is evidence from the history, physical examination, or laboratory findings that the disturbance is the direct pathophysiological consequence of another medical condition.
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            | The disturbance is not better explained by another mental disorder (e.g., a manic episode).
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize The disturbance does not occur exclusively during the course of a delirium.
        li.APP-Item.SpaceAbove.SpaceBelow
          p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
            | The disturbance causes clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other important areas of functioning.
      .APP-CodeNote
        p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
          b Coding note:
          |  Include the name of the medical condition in the name of the mental disorder (e.g., 293.89 [F06.1]) catatonic disorder due to hepatic encephalopathy). The other medical condition should be coded and listed separately immediately before the catatonic disorder due to the medical condition (e.g., 572.2 [K71.90] hepatic encephalopathy; 293.89 [F06.1] catatonic disorder due to hepatic encephalopathy).
    .APP-DiagnosticFeatures(id='x92567.2434720')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2418018') Diagnostic Features
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | The essential feature of c
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName atatonic disorder due to another medical condition
        |  is the presence of catatonia that is judged to be attributed to the physiological effects of another medical condition. Catatonia can be diagnosed by the presence of at least three of the 12 clinical features in Criterion A. There must be evidence from the history, physical examination, or laboratory findings that the catatonia is attributable to another medical condition (Criterion B). The diagnosis is not given if the catatonia is better explained by another mental disorder (e.g., m
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName anic episode
        | ) (Criterion C) or if it occurs exclusively during the course of a d
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName elirium
        |  (Criterion D).
    .APP-AssociatedFeatures(id='x92567.2434741')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2434744') Associated Features Supporting Diagnosis
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | A variety of medical conditions may cause catatonia, especially neurological conditions (e.g., neoplasms, head trauma, cerebrovascular disease, encephalitis) and metabolic conditions (e.g., hypercalcemia, hepatic encephalopathy, homocystinuria, diabetic ketoacidosis)
        span.APP-CiteGroup
          | (
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHGADCH') Caroff et al.
            |  2004
          | ;
          span.APP-Cite
            a.APP-CrossRefEndnote(href='#CIHJEDBJ') Fink
            |  and Taylor 2003
          | )
        | . The associated physical examination findings, laboratory findings, and patterns of prevalence and onset reflect those of the etiological medical condition.
    .APP-DifferentialDiagnosis(id='x92567.2418139')
      h4.Head(id='x92567.2418045') Differential Diagnosis
      p.APP-Para.FirstLineIndent.CellChangeDefaultSize
        | A separate diagnosis of c
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName atatonic disorder due to another medical condition
        |  is not given if the catatonia occurs exclusively during the course of a d
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName elirium or neuroleptic malignant syndrome.
        | If the individual is currently taking neuroleptic medication, consideration should be given to
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName medication-induced movement disorders
        |  (e.g., abnormal positioning may be due to n
        span.APP-DSMDisorderName euroleptic-induced acute dystonia
        | ) or neuroleptic malignant syndrome (e.g., catatonic-like features may be present, along with associated vital sign and/or laboratory abnormalities). Catatonic symptoms may be present in any of the following five psychotic disorders: brief psychotic disorder, schizophreniform disorder, schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, and substance/medication-induced psychotic disorder. It may also be present in some of the neurodevelopmental disorders, in all of the bipolar and depressive disorders, and in other mental disorders.
